{"name":"Naga Viper Pepper","hex":"#ed292b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"naga","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A loincloth."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A member of a class of semi-divine creatures, often taking the form of a very large snake and associated with water.","A fictional creature with the lower body of a snake, and an upper body either of an anthropomorphic version thereof, or of a human or another humanoid creature. The serpentine equivalent of a taur."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/naga"},{"term":"viper","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A venomous snake in the family Viperidae.","Any venomous snake."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/viper"},{"term":"pepper","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant of the family Piperaceae.","A spice prepared from the fermented, dried, unripe berries of this plant."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To add pepper to.","To strike with something made up of small particles."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pepper"}],"entries":[{"title":"Naga Viper pepper","blocks":[{"text":"The Naga Viper pepper is a hot chili pepper developed in England. In 2011, it was recorded as the \"World's Hottest Chili\" by the Guinness World Records with a rating of 1,382,118 Scoville heat units (SHU), but was surpassed in SHU by the Carolina Reaper, in 2017, and again by the latest world record holder Pepper X in 2023."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Naga_Viper_486_orig.jpg/330px-Naga_Viper_486_orig.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naga_Viper_pepper","wikidata":"Q642692"},{"title":"Viper","blocks":[{"text":"Vipers are snakes in the family Viperidae, found in most parts of the world, except for Antarctica, Australia, Hawaii, Madagascar, Ireland, and various other isolated islands. All vipers are venomous, and have long, hinged fangs that permit deep envenomation of their prey. Three subfamilies are currently recognized. They are also known as viperids. The name \"viper\" is derived from the Latin word vipera, -ae, also meaning viper, possibly from vivus (\"living\") and parere, referring to the trait viviparity common in vipers like most of the species of Boidae. The earliest known vipers are believed to have diverged from the rest of the clade Caenophidia in the early Eocene."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Viperidae-01.jpg/330px-Viperidae-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viper","wikidata":"Q163656"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Crotalus_skull.jpg/500px-Crotalus_skull.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Crotalus_skull.jpg/500px-Crotalus_skull.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A rattlesnake skull, showing the long fangs used to inject venom","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrotalus_skull.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Arabian_Horned_viper.jpg/500px-Arabian_Horned_viper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Arabian_Horned_viper.jpg/1280px-Arabian_Horned_viper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Arabian_Horned_viper.jpg/500px-Arabian_Horned_viper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Arabian Horned Viper from Arabian Peninsula","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArabian_Horned_viper.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2